sections.body:
The notification service sends an SMS for every authorized payment, and each send takes about 200 ms because the SMS provider is slow. The `notifications` topic has 6 partitions, so the service's consumer group can use at most 6 consumers (lesson 7): one owner per partition, and a seventh consumer sits idle. Six consumers at 5 sends a second each make 30 a second. On a sale day payments reach 300 a second, and the backlog grows by 270 every second.

Each consumer-group fix hurts. More partitions: 60 of them for 60 consumers, chosen up front because adding partitions later moves keys (lesson 4), and nearly empty for most of the year. Threads inside each consumer: possible, but the bookmark is one offset per partition, and it can only move past a record when every record before it is done, so you end up tracking finished offsets by hand (lesson 6). And a record that keeps failing still blocks its partition (lesson 8).

What the notification service wants is a queue. Any free worker takes the next message and acknowledges it when it's done, and a message whose worker died goes to someone else. Order doesn't matter: two texts for two payments can go out either way round.

A **share group** gives a topic those semantics. Its members don't own partitions: they all read from all of them, and the broker hands out records one by one, so several members read one partition at the same time and the partition count no longer caps the number of consumers. You use `KafkaShareConsumer` instead of `KafkaConsumer`: the same `subscribe` and `poll`, and no assignment, `seek` or offset of your own. The topic doesn't change. A consumer group and a share group can read it at the same time, each with its own progress.

```java
try (var consumer = new KafkaShareConsumer<String, String>(Map.of(
        "bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092",
        "group.id", "notify",                               // a share group: no partitions of its own
        "key.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class, "value.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class))) {
    consumer.subscribe(List.of("notifications"));
    while (running) {
        for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)))
            sms.send(r.value());                            // implicit mode: the next poll accepts this batch
    }
}
```

```kotlin
KafkaShareConsumer<String, String>(mapOf(
    "bootstrap.servers" to "localhost:9092",
    "group.id" to "notify",                                 // a share group: no partitions of its own
    "key.deserializer" to StringDeserializer::class.java, "value.deserializer" to StringDeserializer::class.java,
)).use { consumer ->
    consumer.subscribe(listOf("notifications"))
    while (running) {
        for (r in consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)))
            sms.send(r.value())                             // implicit mode: the next poll accepts this batch
    }
}
```

Share groups came with KIP-932: early access in Kafka 4.0, a preview in 4.1, production-ready since 4.2. The Try-it runs both kinds of group on a topic with one partition.

